---
import TopicLayout from "../../layouts/TopicLayout.astro";
import { loadMirror } from "../../lib/mirror";
import { capacityLabel, dateLabel } from "../../lib/facts";
import { texasProjects as researchProjects, projectPath } from "../../lib/research";
const mirror = await loadMirror();
const facilities = mirror.facilities.facilities.filter((f: any) => f.state === "TX");
const projects = mirror.announcements.announcements.filter((p: any) => p.state === "TX").sort((a: any, b: any) => (b.reported_capacity_mw ?? -1) - (a.reported_capacity_mw ?? -1));
const total = projects.reduce((sum: number, p: any) => sum + (p.reported_capacity_mw ?? 0), 0);
const permit = "https://records.tceq.texas.gov/cs/idcplg?IdcService=TCEQ_EXTERNAL_SEARCH_GET_FILE&dID=8600163&Rendition=Web";
const inventory = projects[0]?.source?.url;
---
<TopicLayout title="Texas BTM Data Center Power Projects | Behind the Watt" description="Texas behind-the-meter data center power: Abilene's permits, turbine ratings and a dated project pipeline, with source links and coverage limits." path="/states/texas/" heading="Texas behind-the-meter data center power projects" deck="Start with the air record. Follow the equipment, the legal entity and the dates before treating a proposed campus as available power.">
  <section class="topic-section wrap">
    <div class="topic-two">
      <div><h2>What is documented in Texas?</h2>
        <p>Start with the reviewed Abilene equipment dossier and {researchProjects.length} additional project research dossiers below. Crusoe Stargate Abilene is identified in the air record as Longhorn Data Center, operated by Abilene DC 1, LLC. Its January 2025 permit review specifies 360.5 MW on a permitted, site-rated basis. Current metered delivery to computing is not established.</p>
        <p>The broader inventory below contains {projects.length} Texas entries with {(total / 1000).toFixed(1)} GW of reported capacity from a third-party snapshot dated April 30, 2026. It is a research starting list. The snapshot's project statuses and expected dates have not been reverified here.</p>
      </div>
      <aside class="topic-note"><h3>Two evidence layers</h3><p>The reviewed facility dossier and the dated announcement inventory have different coverage, dates and capacity definitions. Do not add them together or use their ratio as a completion rate.</p><p><a href="/guides/data-center-power-capacity/">How to read different MW claims</a></p></aside>
    </div>
    <div class="topic-table" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Texas facility dossiers">
      <table><caption>BTW's published Texas dossiers · export {dateLabel(mirror.summary.as_of)}</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Facility</th><th scope="col">Documented equipment</th><th scope="col">Evidence boundary</th></tr></thead><tbody>
        {facilities.map((f: any) => <tr><td><a href={`/facility/${f.slug}/`}>{f.name}</a><small>{f.county} County · {f.developer}</small></td><td>{capacityLabel(f)} MW<small>Permitted site ratings</small></td><td>TCEQ 177263 and O4721<small>No current output measurement published</small></td></tr>)}
      </tbody></table>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="topic-section wrap">
    <h2>Three more Texas projects, with different power boundaries</h2>
    <p>These selected primary-source dossiers distinguish regulator evidence, developer reports and open questions. They are separate research coverage and add no MW to the reviewed facility fleet or historical inventory.</p>
    <div class="topic-table" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Additional Texas project research"><table><caption>Sources checked October 27, 2026 · selected coverage, not a statewide census</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Project</th><th scope="col">Capacity boundary</th><th scope="col">Evidence basis</th></tr></thead><tbody>{researchProjects.map(project => <tr><td><a href={projectPath(project.slug)}>{project.name}</a><small>{project.county}</small></td><td>{project.capacity}</td><td>{project.basis}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div>
    <p><a href="/changes/">Follow the source-linked change log</a>, including Abilene's permit revision, or <a href="/api/v1/research-projects.json">download the research records</a>.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="topic-section wrap">
    <h2>Abilene: a permit trail you can follow</h2>
    <p>The original registration technical review records an August 15, 2024 receipt date. The later modification was received January 11, 2025 and approved January 22. These are different milestones: the modification date is not the first filing date.</p>
    <p>The <a href={permit}>177263 modification technical review</a> changes the fleet from six to five turbines of each model. Five LM2500 units at 34.1 MW and five Titan 350 units at 38 MW give 170.5 + 190 = 360.5 MW. The review describes onsite use for data centers and computing. The separate <a href="https://www.tceq.texas.gov/assets/public/permitting/air/publicnotice/37589sop.pdf">O4721 federal operating permit</a> was issued January 16, 2026.</p>
    <p>Crusoe's <a href="https://www.crusoe.ai/resources/newsroom/crusoe-announces-flagship-abilene-data-center-is-live">September 30, 2025 announcement</a> establishes that the data center was live by that report. It does not establish the exact first day of BTM generation or measured power from every permitted unit.</p>
    <p><a href="/equipment/lm2500-titan-350/">Read the turbine-by-turbine permit breakdown</a> or open the <a href="/facility/crusoe-stargate-abilene/">complete Abilene evidence dossier</a>, including receipts and event dates.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="topic-section wrap">
    <h2>Texas project pipeline: the dated announcement layer</h2>
    <p>This is the Texas subset of the Environmental Integrity Project inventory carried in BTW's open data. Names, technologies, status and expected years below are <strong>as reported on April 30, 2026</strong>, including entries whose expected dates may now have passed. They are not confirmations of present construction, equipment orders, permits or operation. Capacities can describe different project phases and boundaries.</p>
    <p class="topic-small"><a href={inventory}>Original EIP spreadsheet</a> · <a href="/api/v1/announcements.json">Full dated JSON with per-entry provenance</a> · Published facility evidence appears separately above.</p>
    <details><summary>Show all {projects.length} historical Texas project entries</summary>
      <div class="topic-table" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Historical Texas project announcements">
        <table><caption>Historical third-party snapshot · {dateLabel(projects[0]?.source_as_of)} · not a current operating fleet</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Reported project / county</th><th scope="col">Reported MW</th><th scope="col">Status at snapshot</th><th scope="col">Expected operation at snapshot</th></tr></thead><tbody>
          {projects.map((p: any) => <tr><td>{p.name}<small>{p.county || "County not reported"} · {p.generating_technology || "Technology not reported"}</small></td><td>{p.reported_capacity_mw == null ? "Unknown" : p.reported_capacity_mw.toLocaleString("en-US")}</td><td>{p.operating_status}</td><td>{p.expected_operating_year || "Unknown"}</td></tr>)}
        </tbody></table>
      </div>
    </details>
  </section>
  <section class="topic-section wrap">
    <h2>How to qualify a Texas project</h2>
    <ol><li><strong>Match the legal entity and location.</strong> Marketing names can differ from the permit applicant. Abilene DC 1 / Longhorn is an example; a name match alone is insufficient.</li><li><strong>Identify the document stage.</strong> A filing, technical review, issued authorization and operating record answer different questions. Keep the first application and later amendments separate.</li><li><strong>Check the equipment boundary.</strong> Separate primary onsite gas generation, emergency backup and grid capacity. Track the phase, model, count, site rating and hours together.</li><li><strong>Check commercial relevance directly.</strong> A permit naming equipment is not evidence of an open procurement opportunity. Verify scope and participants before treating a project as a sales lead.</li></ol>
    <p>To model economics after establishing scope, use the <a href="/power-cost-calculator/">gas generation cost calculator</a>. Replace the illustrative assumptions with your project's fuel, utilization, installed cost and tariff inputs.</p>
    <h3>Coverage and source dates</h3><p class="topic-small">Abilene primary-record review: October 27, 2026. Announcement snapshot: April 30, 2026. The public export date is not a claim that every entry was rechecked that day. See <a href="/#method">source coverage and retrieval limits</a>. The original registration review is linked in the dossier's source receipts.</p>
  </section>
</TopicLayout>
